Planning-Quality Degradation in Long-Horizon Autonomous 
Research Agents 
Abstract 
Large language models (LLMs) have developed from passive text generators into increasingly autonomous 
agents capable of browsing the web, invoking software tools, maintaining memory, decomposing objectives, 
and executing multi-step workflows. These capabilities are particularly promising for autonomous research 
agents, whose tasks may involve discovering literature, formulating search strategies, collecting evidence, 
reconciling conflicting sources, synthesizing findings, and producing a defensible research report. However, 
performance that is strong on individual reasoning steps does not necessarily translate into reliable 
performance over long horizons. This paper examines planning-quality degradation: the systematic 
deterioration of an agent's ability to maintain coherent, evidence-grounded, goal-directed plans as the number 
of interdependent actions increases. 
The paper synthesizes research on LLM planning, tool use, reasoning-and-acting, reflection, memory, long
context processing, autonomous-agent benchmarks, and web-based research. Existing evidence indicates 
several interacting degradation mechanisms: local or myopic decision-making, error propagation, context 
overload, imperfect memory retrieval, goal drift, unreliable tool selection, premature commitment, weak 
verification, and vulnerability to environmental perturbations. Benchmarks such as WebArena, AgentBench, 
GAIA, BrowseComp, and τ-bench demonstrate that realistic multi-step tasks remain substantially more 
difficult than isolated question answering. Approaches including ReAct, Tree of Thoughts, LLM+P, 
Reflexion, skill libraries, external memory, and structured research workflows mitigate individual failure 
modes but do not yet provide a unified solution. 
The paper argues that future autonomous research systems should treat planning as a continuously evaluated 
process rather than a one-time decomposition of a user objective. Promising directions include hierarchical 
and adaptive planning, explicit state representations, plan-quality estimators, uncertainty-aware search, 
evidence provenance graphs, execution checkpoints, selective replanning, and evaluation metrics that 
measure degradation as a function of horizon length. A central research challenge is therefore not merely 
increasing model intelligence, but developing agent architectures whose planning quality remains stable as 
tasks become longer, more uncertain, and more dynamically interactive. 
Keywords: autonomous agents; large language models; long-horizon planning; planning quality; research 
agents; tool use; memory; reasoning; agent evaluation; information retrieval 
1. Introduction 
The emergence of large language model (LLM)-based autonomous agents has changed the scope of 
machine-assisted problem solving. Rather than producing an answer from a single prompt, an agent can now 
formulate intermediate objectives, search external information, invoke tools, inspect their outputs, revise its 
approach, and execute actions over an extended trajectory. Surveys of LLM-based autonomous agents 
describe this development as a transition from conventional language generation toward systems capable of 
perception, reasoning, planning, action, and feedback-driven adaptation (Wang et al., 2023; Huang et al., 
2024). 
Autonomous research is a particularly demanding application. A research task rarely consists of one 
information-retrieval operation. Instead, an agent may need to interpret a broad research question, identify 
subquestions, locate authoritative sources, distinguish primary from secondary evidence, compare competing 
