findings, resolve contradictions, maintain citations, and synthesize the evidence into a coherent conclusion. 
The resulting workflow is both long-horizon and state-dependent: later decisions depend on what was 
discovered earlier, while early mistakes can constrain the quality of all subsequent steps. 
This creates an important distinction between reasoning quality at a single step and planning quality across a 
trajectory. An LLM may correctly reason about an individual document yet choose an ineffective search 
query. It may retrieve an appropriate source but fail to remember why that source was relevant several steps 
later. It may generate a plausible intermediate conclusion and then unconsciously use that conclusion to bias 
subsequent searches. Thus, an agent can exhibit high local competence while producing a globally poor 
research process. 
This phenomenon is referred to in this paper as planning-quality degradation (PQD). PQD is defined as the 
decline in the quality, coherence, relevance, efficiency, or reliability of an agent's planned behavior as the 
temporal horizon and dependency structure of a task increase. The concept is broader than ordinary 
execution failure. An execution failure occurs when an action does not achieve its intended effect; planning 
degradation occurs when the agent's representation of what should be done next and why becomes 
progressively less aligned with the overall objective. 
Several findings motivate this perspective. AgentBench identified poor long-term reasoning, decision
making, and instruction following as important obstacles for usable LLM agents across multiple interactive 
environments (Liu et al., 2024). WebArena demonstrated that realistic web tasks involving multiple 
interactions remain difficult even for powerful models; its reported GPT-4-based baseline achieved only 
14.41% end-to-end success compared with 78.24% human performance (Zhou et al., 2024). Similarly, GAIA 
showed a large gap between human performance and GPT-4 with plugins on real-world assistant tasks 
requiring reasoning, browsing, multimodality, and tool use (Mialon et al., 2024). 
The central argument of this paper is that long-horizon autonomous research should be understood as a 
planning stability problem as much as a reasoning problem. Increasing model scale or context length can 
improve individual capabilities, but these improvements do not automatically guarantee stable planning over 
hundreds of dependent actions. Indeed, research on long-context language modeling has shown that simply 
providing a model with more context does not ensure that it will reliably use all relevant information (Liu et 
al., 2024). 
2. Background and Related Work 
Traditional autonomous-agent research conceptualizes an agent as a system that observes an environment, 
maintains a state representation, selects actions, and seeks to maximize an objective. LLM agents retain this 
basic structure but replace or augment conventional symbolic planners, policies, or controllers with a 
language model capable of interpreting natural-language objectives and generating actions. 
Wang et al. (2023) provide a broad survey of LLM-based autonomous agents and identify recurring 
components including profiling, memory, planning, action generation, and interaction with environments. 
Huang et al. (2024) specifically organize LLM-agent planning research around task decomposition, plan 
selection, external modules, reflection, and memory. ReAct interleaves reasoning and external actions, while 
Toolformer and WebGPT demonstrate the value of tool use and browser interaction (Yao et al., 2023; Schick 
et al., 2023; Nakano et al., 2022). 
Tree of Thoughts (Yao et al., 2023) extends sequential reasoning by allowing multiple candidate reasoning 
paths, evaluation, and backtracking. LLM+P (Liu et al., 2023) instead delegates formal planning to classical 
planners after translating natural-language objectives into PDDL. These approaches establish that language 
competence and planning competence are related but distinguishable capabilities. 
