Reflexion (Shinn et al., 2023) introduces verbal reinforcement learning in which feedback from unsuccessful 
attempts is stored in an episodic memory buffer. Such memory is particularly important for long-horizon 
research, but memory can itself become a source of degradation when irrelevant or incorrect information is 
retrieved or when important evidence is buried in a large context. 
3. Planning-Quality Degradation in Long-Horizon Research 
Planning quality is multidimensional. For a research trajectory T=(a_1,…,a_H) of horizon H, it can be 
conceptualized as Q(T)=αC+βE+γR+δV−λK, where C represents coherence with the global research 
objective, E evidence quality and coverage, R relevance, V verification and factual reliability, and K 
unnecessary cost. 
Several interacting mechanisms cause degradation. First, myopic decision-making can favor actions with 
high immediate utility over actions that improve the overall research trajectory. Recent planning-centric 
work argues that step-wise reasoning can behave like a greedy policy as the horizon increases (Wang et al., 
2026). 
Second, errors compound. Under a simplified independence assumption, if every dependent step has 
correctness probability p, then P(success)=p^H. Even small per-step weaknesses can therefore become severe 
over long trajectories. 
Third, context overload can produce effective state loss. Liu et al. (2024) documented the “lost in the 
middle” phenomenon, showing that models may fail to use relevant information reliably when it is embedded 
in long contexts. For research agents, this can result in repeated searches, contradictory summaries, or 
excessive dependence on recent information. 
Fourth, goal drift can occur when early search results, intermediate conclusions, memory summaries, or tool 
affordances progressively narrow the agent's interpretation of the original research objective. Fifth, tool use 
enlarges the action space and introduces new failure modes. AgentDojo demonstrates that tool-mediated 
environments can expose agents to prompt injection and other security problems (Debenedetti et al., 2024). 
Finally, premature commitment can cause a research agent to select a hypothesis or evidence path before 
sufficient evidence has been collected. Search-based methods such as Tree of Thoughts mitigate this by 
preserving multiple candidate trajectories. 
4. Current Approaches and Developments 
Current mitigation strategies include receding-horizon planning, hierarchical planning, search-based 
deliberation, external planners, reflection, persistent memory, and improved evaluation environments. 
Receding-horizon approaches avoid generating extremely long plans in advance. The agent executes a short 
sequence, observes the environment, and replans. ReAct follows a related principle by interleaving reasoning 
and action. Hierarchical planning decomposes a global objective into research questions, evidence categories, 
and concrete retrieval or verification operations. 
Search-based deliberation allows multiple candidate plans to be explored and evaluated. LLM+P 
demonstrates that classical planners can provide strong formal planning capabilities when connected to 
language models. Reflexion provides a mechanism for learning from previous failures, while Voyager 
demonstrates the potential of persistent executable skill libraries. 
Evaluation has also advanced. WebArena evaluates realistic multi-site web tasks; AgentBench evaluates 
agents across multiple interactive environments; GAIA focuses on general assistant tasks requiring 
reasoning, multimodality, browsing, and tools. BrowseComp emphasizes persistent browsing and difficult 
