information discovery, while τ-bench evaluates dynamic tool-agent-user interaction and exposes variability 
across repeated trials. 
5. Research Challenges and Limitations 
A major limitation of current evaluation is its focus on final task success rather than the trajectory. A stronger 
evaluation should measure goal alignment, evidence coverage, source authority, redundancy, plan revisions, 
contradiction rate, memory retrieval, tool selection, and recovery time throughout the task. 
Failure attribution is another challenge because retrieval, reasoning, planning, memory, tool execution, and 
verification errors are often entangled. Long-horizon research also requires uncertainty calibration: an agent 
should explicitly distinguish known, supported, uncertain, and contradictory claims. 
Research environments are dynamic and potentially adversarial. Web pages change, APIs fail, documents 
disappear, and retrieved content can contain misleading instructions. Long-horizon deliberation is also 
computationally expensive because search, reflection, verification, and repeated model calls increase latency 
and cost. 
Finally, benchmark contamination remains a concern because web-based benchmark information can enter 
training corpora. Dynamic tasks, held-out sources, temporal splits, and procedurally generated research 
questions can help distinguish genuine research ability from memorization. 
6. Research Gaps and Future Directions 
The field lacks a unified theory of planning-quality degradation in LLM agents. Future work should model 
planning quality as a function of horizon, uncertainty, branching factor, memory load, and environmental 
volatility. 
Horizon-adaptive planning is promising: agents could allocate deeper planning to decisions with high 
uncertainty or downstream impact while handling routine decisions cheaply. Evidence-centric planning could 
represent claims, sources, datasets, methods, and observations in an evidence graph whose edges encode 
support, contradiction, derivation, and verification requirements. 
Memory should become provenance-aware, preserving the source, evidence location, context, confidence, 
and date associated with each stored claim. Independent plan critics and verification modules could reduce 
the weakness of self-criticism by separating planning from evaluation. 
Research-specific benchmarks should require agents to formulate a plan, search heterogeneous scholarly 
sources, distinguish primary and secondary evidence, reconcile contradictions, maintain citation provenance, 
revise hypotheses, and justify evidence sufficiency. Evaluation should score the entire trajectory and include 
repeated trials so that both mean performance and variance are measured. 
Finally, human-in-the-loop checkpoints can be introduced at high-impact decisions such as research-question 
interpretation, hypothesis selection, evidence sufficiency, and final synthesis. The objective is not to 
eliminate autonomy but to allocate oversight where planning failure would be most costly. 
7. Conclusion 
Long-horizon autonomous research exposes a fundamental limitation of current LLM-agent systems: strong 
local reasoning does not guarantee stable global planning. As an agent performs more actions, it must 
continually preserve its objectives, retrieve relevant memory, evaluate evidence, select appropriate tools, 
detect errors, revise hypotheses, and decide whether its current plan remains valid. 
