The literature provides strong evidence for individual components of this problem. ReAct demonstrates the 
value of grounding reasoning in environmental feedback; Tree of Thoughts shows the benefits of explicit 
search and backtracking; LLM+P demonstrates the value of delegating planning to formal planners; 
Reflexion illustrates how feedback and memory can improve future behavior; and Voyager demonstrates the 
potential of persistent skill libraries. 
The central research challenge is therefore not simply to make models reason better. It is to construct systems 
whose planning quality remains stable as the horizon grows. This requires explicit state representations, 
adaptive planning depth, evidence-aware memory, reliable verification, uncertainty calibration, selective 
replanning, and evaluation methods that expose degradation throughout a trajectory. 
For autonomous research agents, the ultimate standard should be more demanding than producing a plausible 
final report. A trustworthy system should demonstrate that its research trajectory remained aligned with the 
original question, that important evidence was neither lost nor selectively ignored, that contradictory findings 
triggered appropriate replanning, and that conclusions can be traced to verifiable sources. 
